{"id":"leetcode-118","version":1,"status":"approved","title":"Pascal's Triangle","summary":"Given an integer representing the number of rows, generate and return the first specified number of rows from Pascal's triangle, where each number is the sum of the two numbers directly above it.","statement":"Given an integer representing the number of rows, generate and return the first specified number of rows from Pascal's triangle, where each number is the sum of the two numbers directly above it.","constraints":["1 <= num_rows <= 30.","The first row is [1]; every interior value is the sum of the two above it."],"topics":["arrays","dynamic-programming"],"prerequisites":[],"signature":{"name":"pascal_triangle","parameters":[{"name":"num_rows","annotation":"int","adapter":"raw","source_parameter":null}],"returns":"list[list[int]]","return_adapter":"raw","node_policy":"values","result_mode":"return","mutated_parameter":null,"immutable_parameters":[]},"starter_code":"def pascal_triangle(num_rows: int) -> list[list[int]]:\n    # Write your solution here.\n    pass\n","reference_solution":"def pascal_triangle(num_rows):\n    answer = []\n    for row in range(num_rows):\n        current = [1] * (row + 1)\n        for col in range(1, row):\n            current[col] = answer[-1][col-1] + answer[-1][col]\n        answer.append(current)\n    return answer\n","brute_force_solution":null,"known_wrong_solutions":["def pascal_triangle(num_rows):\n    answer = []\n    for row in range(num_rows - 1):\n        current = [1] * (row + 1)\n        for col in range(1, row):\n            current[col] = answer[-1][col-1] + answer[-1][col]\n        answer.append(current)\n    return answer\n","def pascal_triangle(num_rows):\n    answer = []\n    for row in range(num_rows):\n        current = [1] * (row + 1)\n        for col in range(1, row):\n            current[col] = answer[-1][col-1]\n        answer.append(current)\n    return answer\n"],"target_time_complexity":"O(r²)","target_space_complexity":"O(r²) including output","estimated_minutes":20,"rubric":{"algorithmic_insight":1,"prerequisites":1,"implementation":1,"performance_pressure":0,"edge_cases":0},"hints":["Every row begins and ends with one.","Create the next row from the preceding row's neighboring pairs.","Initialize a row of ones, fill interior index j from previous[j-1]+previous[j], then append it.","Each row must be a separate list so later updates do not change earlier rows."],"editorial":"Build rows in order. Leave the first and last entries as one, and fill interior entries with sums from adjacent positions of the preceding row. The independent oracle uses binomial coefficients instead of this recurrence. There are a quadratic number of returned entries, so quadratic time and output space are necessary.","tests":[{"id":"lc-118-1","arguments":[5],"expected":[[1],[1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,3,1],[1,4,6,4,1]],"comparator":"exact","visible":true,"explanation":"The five rows end in [1,4,6,4,1], with 6 obtained from 3+3 in the previous row.","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-118-2","arguments":[1],"expected":[[1]],"comparator":"exact","visible":true,"explanation":"One row contains only [1].","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-118-3","arguments":[2],"expected":[[1],[1,1]],"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-118-4","arguments":[3],"expected":[[1],[1,1],[1,2,1]],"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-118-5","arguments":[4],"expected":[[1],[1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,3,1]],"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-118-6","arguments":[6],"expected":[[1],[1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,3,1],[1,4,6,4,1],[1,5,10,10,5,1]],"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-118-7","arguments":[7],"expected":[[1],[1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,3,1],[1,4,6,4,1],[1,5,10,10,5,1],[1,6,15,20,15,6,1]],"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"expla